<p> CPI-W			  0.1    0.2	2.7</p>
<p>CPI-W--Index for urban wage earners and clerical workers.</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected a 0.2 percent rise in March consumer prices. Excluding food and energy, they expected a 0.3 percent increase.  </p>
<p>The department said that airline fares rose 4.5 percent in March, partly reflecting the reimposition of the 10 percent federal tax on airline fares.</p>
<p>Tobacco and smoking product prices rose 0.4 percent in March. The department said the 24.9 percent increase in tomato prices last month was the highest since a 33.4 percent increase a year ago. That figure is not seasonally adjusted.</p>
<p>On the energy front, natural gas prices fell by 4.2 percent last month, the biggest decline since the department started keeping records in 1952.</p>
